<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD JATS (Z39.96) Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v1.0 20120330//EN" "JATS-archivearticle1.dtd"><article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Int J Emerg Med</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>International Journal of Emergency Medicine</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="ppub">1865-1372</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1865-1380</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Springer-Verlag</publisher-name><publisher-loc>London</publisher-loc></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19384524</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2657281</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">41</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1007/s12245-008-0041-3</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Clinical Images</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Chest pain in an adolescent male</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Bhagra</surname><given-names>Anjali</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff1">1</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" corresp="yes"><name><surname>Suravaram</surname><given-names>Smitha</given-names></name><address><phone>+1-507-2559099</phone><fax>+1-507-2556592</fax><email>suravaram.smitha@mayo.edu</email></address><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Anderson</surname><given-names>Jana L.</given-names></name><xref ref-type="aff" rid="Aff2">2</xref></contrib><aff id="Aff1"><label>1</label>Department of Internal Medicine, Mayo Clinic College of Medicine, Rochester, MN USA </aff><aff id="Aff2"><label>2</label>Department of Emergency Medicine, Mayo Clinic College of Medicine, Generose G-410, 200 First Street SW, Rochester, MN USA </aff></contrib-group><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>3</day><month>9</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>3</day><month>9</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>9</month><year>2008</year></pub-date><volume>1</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>225</fpage><lpage>226</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>26</day><month>12</month><year>2007</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>30</day><month>6</month><year>2008</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2008</copyright-statement></permissions><custom-meta-group><custom-meta><meta-name>issue-copyright-statement</meta-name><meta-value>&#x000a9; Springer-Verlag London Ltd 2008</meta-value></custom-meta></custom-meta-group></article-meta></front><body><p>A 15-year-old boy was referred to our emergency department for evaluation of subcutaneous emphysema. The patient had presented with gradual onset of dull resting chest pain lasting for almost an hour. The nature of the pain subsequently changed to sharp and radiated to his throat. He had undergone a chest X-ray at the outside emergency department which was remarkable for subcutaneous emphysema. Laboratory evaluation revealed no abnormality. He was subsequently transferred to our emergency department without any further intervention. The patient was symptom free upon arrival. He denied any history of trauma or prior such episodes. Physical examination revealed a well appearing male, alert, oriented, and in no acute distress with palpable subcutaneous emphysema over the right trapezius muscle and neck. A computed tomography (CT) scan of the chest was obtained (Figs.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref> and <xref rid="Fig2" ref-type="fig">2</xref>). Chest CT confirmed the presence of subcutaneous emphysema as seen in Fig.&#x000a0;<xref rid="Fig1" ref-type="fig">1</xref> (arrowhead). There was additional evidence of extensive pneumomediastinum (white arrow). There was no pneumothorax, obvious esophageal or tracheolaryngeal perforation, or fistula.<fig id="Fig1"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;1</label><caption><p>CT confirming subcutaneous emphysema (<italic>arrowhead</italic>) and extensive pneumomediastinum (<italic>arrow</italic>)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12245_2008_41_Fig1_HTML" id="MO1"/></fig><fig id="Fig2"><label>Fig.&#x000a0;2</label><caption><p>Scout view depicting lucency along left mediastinal border (<italic>arrow</italic>) associated with pneumomediastinum. Also note the subcutaneous emphysema in the right chest wall (<italic>arrowhead</italic>)</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="12245_2008_41_Fig2_HTML" id="MO2"/></fig></p><p>Spontaneous mediastinal emphysema (Hamman&#x02019;s syndrome/pneumomediastinum) is a rare condition which is usually benign and nonrecurrent. It is known to be associated with transiently elevated intra-alveolar pressures [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. It may present without any underlying precipitating factors or may be secondary to many factors [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. The commonest presentation is with chest pain and dyspnea, with subcutaneous emphysema being the most common sign [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. A precordial crunching sound synchronous with the heartbeat is the characteristic Hamman&#x02019;s sign [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>], which was absent in our patient. Treatment is expectant [<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="CR1">1</xref>]. The patient was observed for a few hours and subsequently dismissed. The patient did well on follow-up and had an uneventful course.</p></body><back><ack><p><bold>Conflicts of interest</bold> None.</p></ack><ref-list id="Bib1"><title>Reference</title><ref id="CR1"><label>1.</label><mixed-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Newcomb</surname><given-names>AE</given-names></name><name><surname>Clarke</surname><given-names>CP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Spontaneous pneumomediastinum: a benign curiosity or a significant problem?</article-title><source>Chest</source><year>2005</year><volume>128</volume><issue>5</issue><fpage>3298</fpage><lpage>3302</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1378/chest.128.5.3298</pub-id><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16304275</pub-id></mixed-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>